    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to use the fact that the probability of independent events occurring together is the product of their individual probabilities.\n\nGiven:\n- The probability it will rain on Saturday is \\(60\\%\\), which can be written as \\(0.60\\).\n- The probability it will rain on Sunday is \\(25\\%\\), which can be written as \\(0.25\\).\n\nSince the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days is the product of the probabilities of rain on each day. Therefore, we calculate:\n\n\\[\n0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n\\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\n\\[\n0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
