    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to use the fact that the probabilities of independent events are the products of their individual probabilities.\n\n1. Identify the probability of rain on each day:\n   - The probability of rain on Saturday, \\( P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\), is \\( 60\\% \\) or \\( 0.60 \\).\n   - The probability of rain on Sunday, \\( P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) \\), is \\( 25\\% \\) or \\( 0.25 \\).\n\n2. Since the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is the product of the individual probabilities:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Rain on both days}) = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday})\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given probabilities:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Rain on both days}) = 0.60 \\times 0.25\n   \\]\n\n3. Perform the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n   \\]\n\n4. Convert the probability to a percentage by multiplying by 100:\n   \\[\n   0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both days is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
