      "text": "To determine the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we use the fact that the probability of two independent events occurring is the product of their individual probabilities. Here, the probability that it will rain on Saturday is 60% and the probability that it will rain on Sunday is 25%. The probability of both events happening (it raining on both days) is given by:\n\n\\[ P(\\text{Rain on Saturday and Sunday}) = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) \\]\n\nSubstituting the given probabilities into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ P(\\text{Rain on Saturday and Sunday}) = 0.60 \\times 0.25 \\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[ 0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15 \\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\n\\[ 0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both days is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
